<h1>Remediation — Accident &amp; Urgent Care</h1><p>Item <code>accident-emergency</code> (id <code>62296a1920ec663652420a15</code>), EN locale. Assessed against the live Webflow governance: <code>brand-voice.md</code> (v21), <code>house-style.md</code> (v1), <code>medical-compliance.md</code> (v1), <code>cms-fields.md</code> (v1), and the <code>write-service-page</code> skill (v1).</p><p>This is a <strong>general-practice / urgent-care</strong> service page — not cosmetic and not an injectable/device — so several compliance requirements do not apply (see snapshot). Findings are mostly light <strong>spot fixes</strong>; the larger observations are in <em>Presentation &amp; structural notes</em> and are not auto-applied.</p><h2>How to approve</h2><p>Each change below has an ID and a <strong>Decision</strong> field showing <mark>pending</mark>. Change it to <strong>approved</strong> or <strong>rejected</strong>, or just reply with the IDs (e.g. "approve C1–C5, reject C7"). Only approved changes are written to the CMS, as a draft, for you to review and publish. The exact text being changed is wrapped in <code>&lt;mark&gt;</code> — in <strong>Before</strong> it marks what is replaced; in <strong>After</strong> it marks the replacement.</p><h2>Compliance snapshot</h2><table style="min-width: 75px;"><colgroup><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"></colgroup><tbody><tr><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Rule area</p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Status</p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Note</p></th></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Publish gate (placeholders, tokens, raw Markdown)</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Pass</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Body is clean — no <code>[CONFIRM]</code>, shortcodes, or Markdown.</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Terminology (patients not clients)</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Pass</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>No "clients/customers" used.</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Clinic name</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Pass</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Name not abbreviated to "PD" in body.</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>NZ spelling</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Pass</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>"centre", "stabilised", "medical centre" all correct; no US spellings found.</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>House-style mechanics</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Fixes</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Hyphenation and casing — C2, C3.</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Grammar / correctness</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Fixes</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Subject–verb and verb choice — C1, C4.</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Inclusive / translation-ready (NZ concepts on first mention)</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Fix</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>"ACC" unexpanded — C5.</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Cosmetic "Individual results will vary."</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>N/A</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Not a cosmetic treatment.</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Device / Medsafe disclaimer</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>N/A</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Not an injectable/energy-device treatment.</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Pricing / GST clarity</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>N/A</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>No price shown on this page.</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Structure (template-A scaffold)</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Note</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Older free-form layout — acceptable for urgent care; see N2.</p></td></tr></tbody></table><h2>Proposed spot changes</h2><h3>C1 · Subject–verb agreement</h3><p><em>Required · grammar · field </em><code>text</code><em> · rule: general correctness</em> — <strong>Decision:</strong> <mark>pending</mark></p><p><strong>Before:</strong> Your local <mark>doctors is</mark> also highly skilled in managing accidents and injuries.</p><p><strong>After:</strong> Your local <mark>doctor is</mark> also highly skilled in managing accidents and injuries.</p><p><strong>Why:</strong> "doctors is" does not agree. Singular "doctor is" reads best here (the clinic as your local doctor); if you prefer the plural, the alternative is "doctors are".</p><h3>C2 · Hyphenate "follow-up"</h3><p><em>Required · hyphenation · field </em><code>text</code><em> · rule: </em><code>house-style.md</code><em> → Hyphenate</em> — <strong>Decision:</strong> <mark>pending</mark></p><p><strong>Before:</strong> …complete ACC documentation and provide a <mark>follow up</mark> service…</p><p><strong>After:</strong> …complete ACC documentation and provide a <mark>follow-up</mark> service…</p><p><strong>Why:</strong> House style hyphenates "follow-up" as a noun/adjective.</p><h3>C3 · Fix "X-ray/ Ultrasound" spacing and casing</h3><p><em>Required · mechanics · field </em><code>text</code><em> · rule: </em><code>house-style.md</code><em> → rich-text hygiene</em> — <strong>Decision:</strong> <mark>pending</mark></p><p><strong>Before:</strong> …provide splinting for soft tissue injuries or fractures, refer for <mark>X-ray/ Ultrasound</mark>, complete…</p><p><strong>After:</strong> …provide splinting for soft tissue injuries or fractures, refer for <mark>X-ray or ultrasound</mark>, complete…</p><p><strong>Why:</strong> The slash has a space on one side only, and "Ultrasound" is capitalised mid-sentence. "X-ray or ultrasound" reads cleanly and matches sentence case (X-ray keeps its capital as the conventional form).</p><h3>C4 · "a referral done" → "a referral made"</h3><p><em>Required · grammar · field </em><code>text</code><em> · rule: general correctness</em> — <strong>Decision:</strong> <mark>pending</mark></p><p><strong>Before:</strong> …we will ensure your condition is stabilised, wounds are dressed, and a <mark>referral done</mark> directly to the relevant specialty…</p><p><strong>After:</strong> …we will ensure your condition is stabilised, wounds are dressed, and a <mark>referral made</mark> directly to the relevant specialty…</p><p><strong>Why:</strong> "a referral done" is ungrammatical; "made" is the correct collocation.</p><h3>C5 · Spell out "ACC" on first mention</h3><p><em>Recommended · terminology · field </em><code>text</code><em> · rule: </em><code>brand-voice.md</code><em> → be explicit with NZ-specific concepts on first mention</em> — <strong>Decision:</strong> <mark>pending</mark></p><p><strong>Before:</strong> …refer for X-ray or ultrasound, complete <mark>ACC</mark> documentation and provide a follow-up service, including ACC certificates…</p><p><strong>After:</strong> …refer for X-ray or ultrasound, complete <mark>ACC (Accident Compensation Corporation)</mark> documentation and provide a follow-up service, including ACC certificates…</p><p><strong>Why:</strong> "ACC" is a New Zealand-specific concept. Expanding it on first mention helps new migrants and gives translators unambiguous source text; later uses can stay "ACC".</p><h3>C6 · Remove blanket bold on the allied-health paragraph</h3><p><em>Recommended · formatting · field </em><code>text</code><em> · rule: </em><code>brand-voice.md</code><em> / </em><code>house-style.md</code><em> — bold is for the lede and emphasis, not whole paragraphs</em> — <strong>Decision:</strong> <mark>pending</mark></p><p><strong>Before</strong> (the entire paragraph is wrapped in <code>&lt;strong&gt;</code>): <strong>Our medical centre has good relationships with allied health services and medical care institutions including physiotherapists, hand therapists and orthotic services. These people are eager to help support your recovery from your injuries and facilitate your return to work and normal daily living.</strong></p><p><strong>After</strong> (plain body text, no bold): Our medical centre has good relationships with allied health services and medical care institutions including physiotherapists, hand therapists and orthotic services. They are eager to help support your recovery from your injuries and facilitate your return to work and normal daily living.</p><p><strong>Why:</strong> A whole mid-body paragraph set bold reduces readability and isn't a lede. (The "These people" → "They" tweak here is C7 — approve them together or separately.)</p><h3>C7 · "These people" → "They"</h3><p><em>Optional · voice · field </em><code>text</code><em> · rule: </em><code>brand-voice.md</code><em> → warm, credentialed</em> — <strong>Decision:</strong> <mark>pending</mark></p><p><strong>Before:</strong> …physiotherapists, hand therapists and orthotic services. <mark>These people</mark> are eager to help support your recovery…</p><p><strong>After:</strong> …physiotherapists, hand therapists and orthotic services. <mark>They</mark> are eager to help support your recovery…</p><p><strong>Why:</strong> "These people" is vague and slightly off-tone for allied health professionals.</p><h3>C8 · Minor polish (bundled — approve/reject as one)</h3><p><em>Optional · polish · field </em><code>text</code> — <strong>Decision:</strong> <mark>pending</mark></p><ul><li><p>First mention of GP: <strong>Before</strong> "…as well as standard <mark>GP</mark> services." → <strong>After</strong> "…as well as standard <mark>general practice (GP)</mark> services." (brand voice: expand on first mention)</p></li><li><p>Compound adjectives: "<mark>urgent care</mark> trained" → "<mark>urgent-care</mark> trained"; "<mark>soft tissue</mark> injuries" → "<mark>soft-tissue</mark> injuries".</p></li><li><p>Trailing whitespace at the end of the first paragraph removed.</p></li></ul><p><strong>Why:</strong> Small correctness/consistency nits with no change in meaning.</p><h2>Presentation &amp; structural notes (not auto-applied)</h2><p>These are recommendations for a decision, not spot changes. Say the word and any of them can become its own change block.</p><p><strong>N1 · No closing call to action.</strong></p><p>The page ends on hospital-admission detail with no invitation to book or enrol. Brand voice wants service pages to close by pointing to action. Suggested addition: a short closing line such as <em>"If you need urgent care, we're here — walk in or book an appointment at Ponsonby Doctors."</em> This is new copy, so it needs your wording sign-off rather than a silent fix.</p><p><strong>N2 · Does not follow the template-A scaffold.</strong></p><p>It's free-form prose rather than the italic-lede / Key Benefits / What to Expect structure. That is acceptable for an urgent-care service (template-A is aimed at treatment pages), so I recommend <em>not</em> restructuring it as part of remediation. Flagging only.</p><p><strong>N3 · Meta description is a fragment.</strong></p><p><code>meta-description</code> ends "Fast, quality care." — serviceable and within length, so no change proposed; could be tightened to a full clause if you want consistency across the collection.</p><p><strong>N4 · Missing image alt text.</strong></p><p>The hero and all four gallery photos have <code>alt: null</code> — an accessibility and SEO gap. This is an asset-level fix handled deliberately (not bundled into the text changes above); flag if you'd like it added to this item's scope.</p><h2>Next steps</h2><ol><li><p>You set a <strong>Decision</strong> on each change (and tell me on N1/N4 if you want them actioned).</p></li><li><p>I apply the approved changes to the EN item as a <strong>draft</strong> via the CMS API and summarise exactly what was written.</p></li><li><p>You review the draft in Webflow and publish.</p></li><li><p>I record today's date against <code>accident-emergency</code> in <code>REMEDIATE.hd</code>.</p></li></ol><p><strong>Localization note:</strong> C1–C8 change EN body text. On the next <code>localize-collection</code> run, the affected spans in the zh/ko locales (if translated) will need re-checking against the new EN — noted, not done here.</p>